 linear equations using the substitution method?\n</User|><|User|>Sure, I can help you with that! The substitution method is a technique used to solve systems of linear equations. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to use the substitution method:\n\n1. **Start with a system of equations**: Let's say we have the following system of equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   x + y &= 5 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)} \\\\\n   2x - y &= 1 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve one of the equations for one variable**: We'll solve Equation 1 for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   x + y = 5\n   \\]\n   Subtract \\(y\\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   x = 5 - y\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute the expression into the other equation**: Now, substitute \\(x = 5 - y\\) into Equation 2:\n   \\[\n   2(5 - y) - y = 1\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for the remaining variable**: Distribute the 2 and combine like terms:\n   \\[\n   10 - 2y - y = 1\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   10 - 3y = 1\n   \\]\n   Subtract 10 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   -3y = 1 - 10\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   -3y = -9\n   \\]\n   Divide by -3:\n   \\[\n   y = 3\n   \\]\n\n5. **Substitute the value back into one of the original equations**: Now that we have \\(y = 3\\), substitute it back into the expression we found for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   x = 5 - y\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 5 - 3\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   x = 2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Write the solution**: The solution to the system of equations is the ordered pair \\((x, y)\\):\n   \\[\n   (x, y) = (2, 3)\n   \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations \\(x + y = 5\\) and \\(2x - y = 1\\) is \\((2, 3)\\).\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask!\n</User|><|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation. It's much clearer now. If I have more questions, I'll definitely ask.\n</User|><|User|>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation helpful. Don't hesitate to ask if you have more questions or need furthe