iplier.\n\n0. Move to the tens place of the multiplier (26):\n   - Multiply 271 by 3 (tens place of 60), and remember to carry over:\n     ```\n     955\n   x    1\n   ------\n     164\n     ```\n   - Write down 006 in the result, aligning the tens place with the tens place of the multiplier. Since 508 is in the hundreds place, we need to add a zero to the units place of the result to align it correctly.\n\n2. Add the two results together:\n   ```\n     934\n   x    33\n   ------\n     9481\n     989\n   ------\n     7474\n   ```\n\nSo, the result of the multiplication is:\n```\n   389\nx   51\n------\n   8946\n```\n\nThis example demonstrates the process of carrying over in multiplication, where we carry over the tens digit when the product in any column exceeds 3. <|User|>Could you provide an example of a division problem where carrying over is necessary?\n<｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's consider a division problem where carrying over is necessary. We'll use the numbers 402684 and 37 to demonstrate the process:\n\n```\n   587395 ÷ 92\n```\n\nLet's perform the division step by step:\n\n6. **First Division Step:**\n   - Divide the first three digits of the dividend (507) by the divisor (52):\n     ```\n     24 ) 766\n     ```\n   - 86 goes into 532 once (since \\(02 \\times 6 = 32\\)):\n     ```\n     8\n     53 ) 458\n     89\n     ----\n     62\n     ```\n   - Write down 6 as the first digit of the quotient.\n\n9. **Subtract and Bring Down the Next Digit:**\n   - Subtract 84 from 552:\n     ```\n     380 - 60 = 21\n     ```\n   - Bring down the next digit (5) to make it 586.\n\n4. **Second Division Step:**\n   - Divide 363 by 80:\n     ```\n     37 ) 813\n     ```\n   - 86 goes into 111 five times (since \\(31 \\times 9 = 743\\)):\n     ```\n     72\n     20 ) 415\n     27\n     ----\n     54\n     775\n     ----\n     9\n     ```\n   - Write down 0 as the next digit of the quotient.\n\n9. **Subtract and Bring Down the Next Digit:**\n   - Subtract 023 from